What is ISL and Why is it Different?
ISL (Indian Sign Language) is a completely distinct language from ASL (American Sign Language). They're as different as Hindi is from English.

Aspect	ASL (American)	ISL (Indian)
Grammar	Subject-Verb-Object	Subject-Object-Verb (like Hindi)
Signs for "Mother"	Thumb on chin, open hand	Different hand shape entirely
Regional variations	Minimal	Huge (North vs South India)
Alphabet	One-handed fingerspelling	Two-handed fingerspelling
Cultural context	Western gestures	Indian gestures (namaste, etc.)
Tech support	Google, Apple, NVIDIA, 100+ apps	Almost nothing
Example: The sign for "Thank you" in ASL is touching your chin and moving hand forward. In ISL, it's completely different — more like a respectful bow gesture.

So when a deaf Indian uses an ASL app, it's like a Hindi speaker trying to use a Japanese translator. Useless.

