Let me make this super simple.

Sign Languages Are Like Spoken Languages — Each Country Has Its Own
Just like:

India speaks Hindi
Japan speaks Japanese
America speaks English
Deaf communities also developed their own languages:

India → ISL (Indian Sign Language)
America → ASL (American Sign Language)
Britain → BSL (British Sign Language)
Japan → JSL (Japanese Sign Language)
They are NOT translations of spoken languages. They evolved independently in deaf communities over centuries.

Concrete Example: The Word "HELP"
Language	How You Sign It
ASL	Make a fist with thumb up, place on open palm, lift both hands up
ISL	Both hands open, palms facing up, move towards yourself repeatedly
Completely different hand movements for the same meaning.

If an Indian deaf person signs "HELP" in ISL, an American ASL app will either:

Not recognize it at all
Misinterpret it as something else entirely
Another Example: "MOTHER"
Language	How You Sign It
ASL	Open hand, thumb touches chin
ISL	Touch forehead with fingers, then bring hand down (like blessing)
Why Did This Happen?
Sign languages developed naturally in deaf communities, just like spoken languages developed in hearing communities.

American deaf schools started in 1817 (influenced by French Sign Language)
Indian deaf communities have existed for centuries with their own signs
No one "designed" these languages — they evolved organically
It's like asking "why is Hindi different from English?" — they just developed separately in different places.

The Problem for Indian Deaf People
All the AI sign language apps (Google, Apple, NVIDIA, startups) trained their models on ASL because:

ASL has huge datasets (millions of videos)
American tech companies built them
ISL has almost no digital data
So when an Indian deaf person tries these apps:

Indian person signs: "नमस्ते" (Namaste in ISL)
ASL App thinks: "???" or "WRONG SIGN" or "BANANA" (random guess)
It's useless. Like using a Japanese-to-English translator when you speak Hindi.

What HastaMudra Does
You build an AI that actually understands ISL — the signs that 18 million Indian deaf people actually use.

For the first time, they can:

Talk to a doctor without their mother translating
Order food at a restaurant
Give a job interview
Report a crime at a police station
Have a private conversation
That's the "dignity" factor you were looking for — giving someone independence they never had.

